Phase 6a: expired races, and the real finish time

The window. Payments are now read only up to `ends_at`, and the read is
skipped entirely when the window has already closed. A sale made the day
after a race ended is not a customer of that race, and without the clamp the
count would keep climbing after the clock stopped, letting a founder lose
and then quietly win.

Expiry. A racing racer past `ends_at` with fewer than ten customers moves to
`expired`, by compare-and-set on `status = 'racing'` so exactly one run
writes it and announces it. The event is stamped with the close of the
window rather than with the poll that noticed it.

Ordering. The finish branch is checked first and the expiry branch is an
`else if`, so a race that reached ten is a win even when the poller only
notices afterwards. Ten customers is a win whenever it happened.

finished_at. Now the tenth customer's `first_paid_at`, not the reconcile
instant. A poll runs every thirty minutes, so "when we noticed" can be most
of an hour after "when it happened", and a board that records real events
cannot round that away. The `finished` event carries the same instant, so
the timeline and the race end agree.

Eight new tests, including one that a still-open window never expires and
one that a payment after the close is not counted.

